  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Buttercup Blood Bee (Sphecodes ranunculi) is a small, solitary sweat bee in the family Halictidae, named for its preference for buttercup flowers and distinctive coloration. These tiny bees are native to New England and play an important role as pollinators in local ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Red Abdomen",
      "description": "Distinctive reddish or blood-red coloration on the abdomen, particularly visible on the first few segments",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tiny Size",
      "description": "One of the smallest sweat bee species, typically measuring only 4-5mm in length",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Metallic Sheen",
      "description": "Head and thorax display a metallic green or dark sheen that contrasts with the red abdomen",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Buttercup Blood Bees are found in open meadows, fields, and gardens throughout New England where buttercup flowers bloom in spring and early summer.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their small size, these solitary bees are cleptoparasites, meaning females sometimes lay their eggs in the nests of other bee species, allowing their larvae to feed on provisions meant for the host bee's offspring.",
